  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given that $P(A)=0.25,$ $P(B)=0.45,$ and $P(A\\cup B)=0.6,$ find $P(A\\cup {B}^{\u2032}).$\n\n$P(A\\cup {B}^{\u2032})=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ 0.6 & =0.25+0.45-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =0.25+0.45-0.6 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the corresponding Venn diagram and shade the region $A\\cup {B}^{\u2032}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064054/assets/f8fb716593a35fc1f85ab524dc78bf0fa0cd03dc16b0c5ca92a9b2836e6e7c5b.png)\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup {B}^{\u2032})=0.15+0.1+0.4=0.65.\n$$",
